CompTIA A+ Core 2 — Weekly Study Log
Week: October 26 – November 1, 2026
Focus: Application installation, security fundamentals, identities, access control, and common attacks
Learning resources: Professor Messer videos/transcripts, Q&A, active recall
What I studied
Installing and managing applications
I started the week with Professor Messer's Installing Applications lesson (October 26). Main topics included:
- Application compatibility and system requirements, including 32-bit versus 64-bit concerns.
- Per-user versus system-wide installation.
- Installation media, ISO files, and system imaging.
- Local versus cloud-delivered applications and collaboration tools.
- Why repeatable, managed installations matter in business environments.
Security controls and best practices
I moved into the fundamentals of protecting devices, accounts, and networks:
- Physical security versus logical security.
- Firewalls, access-control lists (ACLs), and basic network access restrictions.
- Zero Trust and why being on an internal network does not automatically make access trustworthy.
- Authentication, authorization, MFA, and time-based one-time passwords (TOTP).
- Wireless security and the importance of appropriate access controls.
Enterprise identities and directories
I built on earlier Windows and domain lessons:
- Active Directory domains and centralized user/device management.
- Security groups, permissions, and folder redirection.
- Kerberos authentication at a conceptual level.
- The difference between proving identity and deciding which resources an identity can access.
Threats and attack techniques
I learned to recognize the purpose and impact of different attacks, including:
- Phishing and other social-engineering techniques.
- Malware and compromised endpoints.
- SQL injection and application-related attacks.
- Denial-of-service attacks.
- Insider threats.
- Endpoint detection and response (EDR) as part of security investigation.
Active recall and review
During the week, I used Q&A and recap questions to check whether I understood the mechanism, not just the name of each concept.

Topics that needed more attention:
- Firewall rules versus ACLs.
- Per-user versus system-wide application installations.
- The basic Kerberos authentication flow.
- What an analyst actually does when investigating an EDR alert.
A follow-up review around the beginning of November showed good overall understanding, with the above as targeted points to revisit rather than reasons to restart the whole section.
Takeaways
This week shifted my Core 2 study from operating-system tools toward security decisions: who can access a resource, how that access is verified, and how an organization responds when something malicious happens.

Next: Continue malware-removal procedures and security maintenance, then study device, SOHO network, and browser security.
